---
/**
 * "Crescent" — a plain crescent moon, nothing else. Drops the old
 * accordion-only "Night Patrol" concept's dashed route + waypoint dots
 * deliberately, not by oversight — this six-icon set's "movement/
 * patrol" idea now belongs to Manned Guarding's footprint mark (see
 * that icon's own comment), so keeping a second patrol-route motif
 * here would duplicate meaning across two cards; a standalone crescent
 * reads cleanly as "night" on its own without it.
 *
 * NOT a naive rescale of the old icon's own path formula — a first
 * attempt at doing exactly that (scaling the old `M17 7 A7 7 0 1 0 17
 * 21 A5 5 0 1 1 17 7 Z`-style two-arcs-sharing-both-endpoints
 * construction) rasterised as a uniform-width "C"/bracket shape, not a
 * tapered crescent, in the mandatory check. Root cause, found by
 * checking the actual maths rather than re-guessing coordinates: an
 * SVG arc's radius is silently scaled UP if the two given endpoints
 * are geometrically too far apart for that radius to reach (per the
 * SVG spec's own arc-out-of-range rule) — the old formula's inner arc
 * radius (5, given the same top/bottom endpoints as the outer arc's
 * own valid radius 7) was already below the minimum radius those two
 * points require, so the renderer silently scaled it up to match the
 * OUTER arc's radius, collapsing the intended two-different-radii
 * crescent into two arcs of the SAME effective radius — a symmetric
 * lens/band, not a crescent. (Whether the retired original ever
 * actually rendered as a true crescent, or had this same latent bug
 * the whole time, was never separately confirmed — it was read from
 * source, never rasterised, before being retired outright.)
 *
 * Rebuilt here from real circle-circle intersection geometry instead
 * of guessed endpoints: an outer circle (r=13, centre (18,20)) and a
 * smaller inner circle (r=11, centre (25,20)) that genuinely overlap,
 * with the path's two endpoints computed as the ACTUAL points where
 * those two circles cross (≈(24.93, 9) and (24.93, 31) — real
 * intersection points, not chosen by eye), so neither arc needs any
 * spec-mandated radius correction and the crescent tapers to real
 * points top and bottom rather than staying a constant band width.
 *
 * SOLID FILL, not stroke-only — the one deliberate exception to this
 * whole icon's own `fill="none"` house style, checked empirically
 * rather than assumed either way: with the intersection geometry above
 * genuinely correct, a stroke-only render of the same closed path
 * still rasterised as two visibly separate nested curves converging at
 * the horn tips (both the outer and inner edge of the sliver are real,
 * separately-visible lines when only the outline is stroked) — a solid
 * fill of the identical path reads immediately and cleanly as a single
 * crescent silhouette instead. Matches how a crescent moon is drawn in
 * most real icon sets anyway, and the same small-solid-fill exception
 * this project's stroke-based icons already make elsewhere for shapes
 * that don't read cleanly as pure strokes (Manned Guarding's toe-dots,
 * CctvMonitoring's lens-centre dot) — just applied to the whole shape
 * here rather than one small detail within it.
 *
 * A second, separate mistake was caught before this shipped, worth
 * recording plainly rather than smoothing over: the FIRST attempt to
 * apply the verified two-arc-with-correct-sweep-flags formula above to
 * this actual file transcribed the wrong final sweep-flag by hand (`…
 * A11 11 0 0 0 24.93 9 Z`, flag 0) instead of the one already confirmed
 * correct in isolation (`… A11 11 0 0 1 24.93 9 Z`, flag 1) — a single
 * digit slip that rasterised as a near-solid blob with only a faint
 * dimple, not a crescent, and was NOT obviously a "one digit is wrong"
 * kind of failure to look at. Caught by diffing the exact `d` string
 * actually shipped in this file, byte for byte, against the
 * independently-rendered-and-confirmed-correct test value, rather than
 * re-deriving the geometry again or trusting a second look at the
 * broken render to spot it. The lesson: once a path string has been
 * empirically verified correct in isolation, copy it byte-for-byte
 * into the real file and diff the two — don't retype it from memory or
 * from the surrounding derivation, even a value that was JUST
 * confirmed working two minutes earlier.
 *
 * Colour inherits via `currentColor` (set directly on the path, not
 * relying on the wrapping `<svg>`'s own `fill="none"` default, same as
 * every other small solid-fill element in this icon set).
 */
interface Props {
  class?: string;
}
const { class: className } = Astro.props;
---

<svg
  viewBox="0 0 40 40"
  fill="none"
  stroke="currentColor"
  stroke-width="2"
  stroke-linecap="square"
  stroke-linejoin="miter"
  class={className}
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <path
    d="M24.93 9 A13 13 0 1 0 24.93 31 A11 11 0 0 1 24.93 9 Z"
    fill="currentColor"
    stroke="none"
  />
</svg>
